# PR#379: half fixed, half refused. The half that was a real defect is fixed: a cursor
# write that failed used to send the entry back through the retry ladder and re-run a
# handler that had already committed, which turned a Redis hiccup into a certain
# duplicate rather than a possible one. Making the cursor atomic with the handler is the
# refused half. It cannot be done: the acknowledgement is a Redis call either way, so a
# database transaction cannot contain it, and the handler this finding names as the
# victim, Raw, writes nothing to the database at all, so there is no transaction for a
# cursor to join. Delivery over a stream is at least once, and this layer answers that
# where the answer belongs, in the handlers: a message is deduped by its source id, a
# status only moves forwards, and group and connection state is last write wins. What is
# left is a repeated PROVIDER_EVENT_RECEIVED webhook after a crash in the window between
# a commit and a SET, and a processed-events table to prevent it would cost a write per
# event on the ordering-critical path.
app/services/whatsapp/connector/consumer/shard_worker.rb: Commit event progress atomically with handler effects  # PR#379
# PR#379: real, and answered as far as it can be. The remedy asked for, draining the
# previous mapping before reading the new shards, is possible when the count goes down
# and impossible when it goes up. Going down, the streams above the new range stop being
# written to, so they drain and the code waits for them: that is implemented. Going up,
# the old mapping lives on the streams the new range also uses (2 to 4 shards leaves the
# old events on 0 and 1, which keep receiving new ones), so there is no point at which
# the old mapping is finished and nothing to wait for. A shard count is topology: the
# safe way to change it is to stop the connector, let the consumers finish, and restart.
# What the code does instead of pretending otherwise is bound the damage. Events are
# never lost, because every stream that still holds work is read. The cursor cannot be
# put back, because it advances through a compare in Lua rather than a SET, so an event
# already processed is never handed to a handler twice. What is left is that a session
# which moved streams mid-conversation can have an older event dropped as superseded,
# which is what fresh? does by design.
app/services/whatsapp/connector/consumer/shard_map.rb: Drain old mappings before increasing the shard count  # PR#379
